        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la tía --- \"the aunt.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish swings the Greek word feminine and is done. English could not: aunt comes from Latin amita, the father's sister, which is neither thius nor avunculus. That is three separate ancient words in play for two relatives, and the two languages picked differently every time."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The accent works exactly as it did in tío --- tí-a, two beats, stress on the first."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One thing to be ready for. In everyday speech across much of the Spanish-speaking world, tío and tía get used for people who are not relatives at all, the way English uses dude or mate. That is casual, not neutral; you will hear it long before you need it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Eight words, and a clear picture of two languages choosing different ancestors."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
