        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la prima --- \"the cousin,\" of a girl or woman."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The ending switch again. What makes this one easy is that English already says prima out loud without translating it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A prima donna is the \"first lady\" of an opera company --- the leading singer. The phrase turned into an insult later, but the words are plain: first woman."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Prima facie evidence is evidence that holds up \"at first face,\" on first look. Same prima, same meaning, borrowed whole out of Latin and never naturalised."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So you already knew this word. You knew it as a label for opera singers and legal arguments, and it is the same word you now use for your cousin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ten words --- and the chapter's last. Two families of relatives, named."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
